        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Khartoum</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1821 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">Established in 1821, 24 km north of the ancient city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soba">Soba</a>, by Isma'il Kamil Pasha, third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a>, as an outpost for the Egyptian Army.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">At the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a>, at an elevation of 385 m, with Khartoum North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a> across the rivers.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Egyptian colonial capital from 1823; a centre of caravan trade and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>; the siege of 1884 to 1885 and the death of General Gordon; the Anglo-Egyptian capital rebuilt after 1898; the capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> from 1956</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Khartoum is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and of Khartoum State, and with Khartoum North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a> forms the country's largest urban area.</span></div>
