    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1821</span><span class="rf-text">Isma'il Kamil Pasha establishes Khartoum as an outpost of the Egyptian Army</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1823</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> moves the seat of its colonial government from Wad Madani to Khartoum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1854</span><span class="rf-text">Heavy rains and floods destroy most of the city, which is rebuilt in mud and stone</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1885</span><span class="rf-text">The Mahdists take Khartoum after a siege that began in 1884, and the garrison is annihilated</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text">Anglo-Egyptian forces reoccupy the city, which becomes the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-egyptian-condominium">Anglo-Egyptian Sudan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1903</span><span class="rf-text">Gordon Memorial College opens</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1956</span><span class="rf-text">Khartoum becomes the capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2023</span><span class="rf-text">Fighting between the Sudanese Armed Forces and the Rapid Support Forces begins in the city</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ali of Egypt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ModernEgypt,_Muhammad_Ali_by_Auguste_Couder,_BAP_17996.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Auguste Couder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Khartoum stands where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>, flowing north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a>, flowing west from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tana">Lake Tana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. The place where the two rivers join is known as al-Mogran or al-Muqran, 'The Confluence'. The land is relatively flat, at an elevation of 385 m, and the climate is a hot desert one: average annual rainfall is only 121.3 mm, and the brief rainy season has its maximum in August. The rivers divide the metropolitan area into three parts, Khartoum proper, Khartoum North to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a> to the west, which are linked by bridges.</p><p>In 1821 Khartoum was established 24 km north of the ancient city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soba">Soba</a> by Isma'il Kamil Pasha, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a>, who had just incorporated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> into his realm. It began as an outpost for the Egyptian Army. In 1823 Egypt shifted the seat of its colonial government from Wad Madani to Khartoum, which became a permanent settlement. The origin of the name is uncertain. One folk etymology derives it from the Arabic khurṭūm, 'trunk' or 'hose', probably for the narrow strip of land between the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Niles</a>; Captain J. A. Grant, who reached the city in 1863, thought it came from qurṭum, 'safflower'; and other proposals are the Nubian Agartum and the Beja hartoom, 'meeting'.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-baker.jpg" alt="Samuel Baker" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker">Samuel Baker</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir-Baker.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LeonisRugitur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With its elevation to capital status Khartoum grew quickly into a regional centre of trade, serving as a rest area on the caravan route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. It was also a major focal point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. The British explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker">Samuel Baker</a>, who visited in 1862, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> the industry 'that kept Khartoum going as a bustling town'. Under European pressure Egypt closed the city's public slave market in 1854, although slaves continued to be sold and trafficked in large numbers, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> region and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuba-mountains">Nuba Mountains</a> and down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> from Dinka and Shilluk territories. Khartoum also became the seat of several European consulates and of the Apostolic Vicariate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>.</p><p>After 1898 the city grew rapidly under British administration. The Gordon Memorial College, now the University of Khartoum, opened in 1903, and a new railway to Port <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> was completed in 1919. These developments prompted large migration from rural areas, and by 1930 Khartoum was estimated to have more than 50,000 people. Growth continued after independence. The population rose to 185,000 by 1964 and stood at 334,000 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1973. The first oil pipeline between Khartoum and Port Sudan was completed in 1977.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ahmad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad">Muhammad Ahmad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Ahmad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Not identified, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Khartoum was founded while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> was part of the realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a>, and from 1823 it was the seat of Egypt's colonial government. In 1882 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> took control of the Egyptian government, leaving the administration of Sudan in the hands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>. At the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdist-war">Mahdist War</a> the British attempted to evacuate the Anglo-Egyptian garrisons from Sudan, and Khartoum fell to the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad">Muhammad Ahmad</a>, in January 1885. The city was then largely abandoned, and the Mahdists made the newly established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a> their centre.</p><p>The reconquest of Sudan by Anglo-Egyptian forces in 1898 made Khartoum the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-egyptian-condominium">Anglo-Egyptian Sudan</a>, and it remained the seat of that government until 1956. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Herbert Kitchener</a> became Governor-General in September 1898 and began a programme of reconstruction, and Reginald Wingate held the office from 1899 to 1916. In 1956 the city was designated the capital of an independent Sudan. A military coup on 26 October 2021 left at least 7 dead, and Prime Minister Abdalla Hamdok was arrested and held with other cabinet members in an unknown location.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> Khartoum became a town of mud and stone houses, which replaced houses of thatch and straw after the floods of 1854. After 1898 Kitchener ordered the mosques rebuilt and guaranteed freedom of religion to all citizens, and he prevented Christian missionaries from trying to proselytise the local Muslims. The new Khartoum was laid out on a street plan in the shape of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Jack</a>. Its buildings were modelled on Victorian architecture, with a distinct Middle Eastern influence and Mediterranean-style windows and balconies, and Wingate rebuilt the Governor-General's Palace in red brick with sandstone corners. Khartoum North was established as a garrison with a dockyard and a railhead to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a> remained the most populous part and largely kept its old shape.</p><p>In the 1970s and 1980s hundreds of thousands of refugees came to Khartoum from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and Uganda. Many Eritrean and Ethiopian refugees assimilated, while others settled in large slums on the outskirts, and since the mid-1980s large numbers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a> and Darfur have settled around the city. The Souq al Arabi, spread over several blocks south of the Great Mosque, is its largest open-air market. Mosques predominate among its places of worship, alongside Coptic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Roman Catholic, Greek Orthodox, Presbyterian and other churches. The National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, founded in 1971, holds two Egyptian temples, those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buhen">Buhen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a>, moved to Khartoum when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a> flooded their sites.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon.jpg" alt="Charles George Gordon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_George_Gordon_by_Freres.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Geruzet Frères - Belgian (active c. 1870-1889), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1854 heavy rains and floods destroyed most of the city. On 13 March 1884 troops loyal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad">Muhammad Ahmad</a> began the siege of Khartoum against the Egyptian garrison led by the British General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a>. The city was fortified by trenches and by a wall connecting the Blue and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Niles</a>, but the Mahdists conquered it on 26 January 1885 and the entire garrison was annihilated. Many of the inhabitants were massacred or enslaved, the survivors were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a>, and Khartoum was largely destroyed and abandoned.</p><p>In 1973 members of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-september">Black September</a> held 10 hostages at the Saudi Arabian embassy, five of them diplomats; the US ambassador, the US deputy ambassador and the Belgian chargé d'affaires were murdered. In November 1991 the government of President Omar al-Bashir sought to remove half the city's population, mostly southern Sudanese deemed squatters; about 425,000 people were placed in five 'Peace Camps' in the desert. On 3 June 2019 the Khartoum massacre took place: over 100 dissidents were killed by soldiers of the Rapid Support Forces, or 61 by the government's count, during protests calling for a civilian government.</p><p>On 15 April 2023 fighting between the Sudanese Armed Forces and the RSF broke out across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, including in Khartoum, at the presidential palace, the RSF's headquarters and the airport. The Sudanese Armed Forces regained full control of the city on 26 March 2025, leaving widespread destruction.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Khartoum was largely destroyed in 1885, and what stands from the next period includes the street plan, the rebuilt mosques, the Governor-General's Palace and Gordon Memorial College, now the University of Khartoum. Its architecture reflects the history of the city since the early 1820s and is marked by native Sudanese, Turkish, British and modern buildings. The Republican Palace Museum stands in the former Anglican All Saints' cathedral beside the old Presidential Palace, and the National Botanical Garden in the Mogran district is one of the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p>As capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, Khartoum hosted the Organization of African Unity summit of 18 to 22 July 1978, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> summit of January 2006 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a> summit of March 2006. In July 2020 activists demanded that al-Zibar Basha street be renamed, after al-Zubayr Rahma Mansur, a slave trader; the street leads to the military base where the 2019 massacre took place. Literary works and Arabic poetry have taken the city's movement, political instability and social change as their subject.</p><p>Khartoum is today the capital and most populous city of Sudan and of Khartoum State. With Khartoum North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a> it forms Greater Khartoum, the largest urban area in Sudan, with an estimated population of 7.1 million people.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Khartoum, at the meeting of the Blue and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Niles</a>, was founded in 1821 as an Egyptian army outpost. It fell to the Mahdists in 1885, was rebuilt as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-egyptian-condominium">Anglo-Egyptian Sudan</a> after 1898, and became the capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in 1956.</em></p>

